Here is a simple example of a fractal heart using p5.js and WebGL. This fractal is created using the Barnsley Fern algorithm, a type of iterative function system (IFS) that can be used to generate fractals.

```javascript
let heart = [];
let numPoint = 100000;

function setup() {
  createCanvas(800, 800, WEBGL);
  stroke(255, 0, 0);
  strokeWeight(2);
  fill(255, 0, 0, 50);
  for (let i = 0; i < numPoint; i++) {
    let x = random(-1, 1);
    let y = random(-1, 1);
    heart.push({x, y});
  }
}

function draw() {
  background(0);
  translate(width / 2, height / 2);
  rotateY(frameCount * 0.01);
  rotateX(frameCount * 0.01);
  for (let i = 0; i < numPoint - 1; i++) {
    let Xu = heart[heartIndex(i, numPoint)];
    let Xu1 = heart[heartIndex((i + 1) % numPoint, numPoint)];
    beginShape();
    vertex(Xu.x, Xu.y, 0);
    bezierVertex(Xu.x + Xu.x * 0.12, Xu.y - Xu.y * 0.4, Xu1.x + Xu1.x * 0.12, Xu1.y - Xu1.y * 0.4, Xu1.x, Xu1.y, 0);
    endShape();
  }
}

function heartIndex(i, n) {
  if (i < n / 2) {
    return i;
  } else {
    return i - n / 2;
  }
}
```

This code will create a heart shape using the Barnsley Fern algorithm. The `setup` function initializes the canvas and the points of the heart, and the `draw` function draws the heart. The heart is rotated slightly in each frame to create a spinning effect.

The `heartIndex` function is used to get the index of the point in the heart structure. Since the Barnsley Fern algorithm is a circular structure, this function is used to wrap around the end of the structure if the index is larger than the size of the structure.

